<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<object>
  <!-- The three stock footer rows leave the page.

       plone.pageletlayout's footer is three elements, and a derico site has
       an answer for each that the design actually draws:

       · `siteactions` renders the `site_actions` category from
         portal_actions — Sitemap, Accessibility, Contact — as a bare row of
         links. Those are authored content here: the footer is editable
         (`collective.blicca.footerblocks`) and the Actions block
         (`derico.blicca.actionsblock`) puts a category of portal actions on a
         page as designed links, so the stock row renders the same actions a
         second time.
       · `copyright` is Plone's own copyright and GPL notice, and `colophon`
         is the „Powered by Plone" badge. Neither is derico's signature. The
         design closes every page with its own footer band — the wordmark
         line, the postal address, phone, email and the Impressum link (see
         `docs/design/derico.de/site`, `.site-footer`) — and that band is
         authored as footer blocks. Below it, Plone's attribution rows read as
         chrome that escaped the design rather than as part of it.

       HIDDEN in plone.portalfooter, the slot plone.pageletlayout assigns the
       three rows to: `@@manage-layout-viewlets` shows each row as hidden and
       can put it back.

       Nothing about Plone's licence obliges the badge or the copyright row to
       be on the page; the attribution that matters is in the source and the
       package metadata, which this does not touch.

       The uninstall profile un-hides all three, so a site that drops the
       theme gets its stock footer back. -->
  <hidden manager="plone.portalfooter" skinname="Plone Default">
    <viewlet name="plone.pageletlayout.copyright" />
    <viewlet name="plone.pageletlayout.colophon" />
    <viewlet name="plone.pageletlayout.siteactions" />
  </hidden>

  <!-- The lead image leaves the page.

       plone.app.contenttypes renders it in plone.abovecontenttitle, inside
       the content header, which the slot layout renders on every page. A
       derico page opens with what its author composed (a hero, an image
       block); the lead image is the teaser and social-card picture, never
       part of the page itself. The uninstall profile un-hides it. -->
  <hidden manager="plone.abovecontenttitle" skinname="Plone Default">
    <viewlet name="contentleadimage" />
  </hidden>
</object>
